  "conclusions": [
    "HidePanel is not purely a visibility change: it invokes current-control teardown before hiding the platform window.",
    "For an active dirty numeric text edit, HidePanel can parse and store the pending text and deliver EVENT_COMMIT to the application before clearing generic panel fields +0x44 and +0x48.",
    "Normal prior HidePanel processes the active dirty edit and clears its dirty latch before clearing +0x44/+0x48. A hidden panel retaining a numeric selection node alone is not evidence of an uncommitted edit.",
    "The conservative UI policy should require the correctly bound hidden target panel generic fields +0x44 and +0x48 both zero before opening it, prohibit text/keyboard/edit interactions, and require no operator input before automatically hiding an automation-opened panel.",
    "LastInputInfo alone proves neither historical cleanliness nor absence of a pre-existing pending edit; it is useful only alongside the initial context guard and the exact application/runtime guards.",
    "DisplayPanel can establish an active control. Its traced first-control focus path sends native event 0x1c with key 9; the text-control branch initializes focus/selection and does not set the identified dirty latch. This is narrower than a proof about every arbitrary runtime control class."
  ],
